  cites Foreword chapter Sha256

 Symbol lookup and symbolic breakpoints reach the debugger through the key
 handler the console actually calls.

 `sym` and `bp-add` were the last two commands that prompted for something
 and consumed nothing. Both had WORKING IMPLEMENTATIONS WITH NO CALLER:
 `debug-sym-lookup` and `bp-add-symbolic` were defined in `DevDebugger`
 and reached from nowhere, while the menu advertised both. The line each
 prompted for fell through to the address evaluator and moved the cursor.

 So this is a wiring test and it is driven entirely through
 `handle-console-input`, for the same reason `debug-expr` and
 `dev-mem-cmd` are: calling the helpers directly would pass against the
 old arms too.

 A NAME IS NOT AN EXPRESSION, and that is the reading this test exists to
 pin. The write value added earlier goes through `ExprEval`; these two
 must not. `parse-expr` handed to an evaluator answers "unknown name" for
 something that was never meant to be one, and the failure would look like
 a broken symbol table rather than a misrouted line.

 THE INDEX IS POPULATED ON PURPOSE. `dev-console-init` starts with
 `empty-source-index` -- the real console fills it after boot -- and
 against an empty index every query answers "No symbols matching", so the
 hit and the miss would be the same line and neither would mean anything.
 The test seeds `index-full-dev-surface`, which is the same canned surface
 the console indexes, so `parse` finds definitions and
 `zzz-no-such-symbol` does not.

 WHY NO BREAKPOINT IS EVER SUCCESSFULLY SET HERE. `bp-add-symbolic` finds
 the symbol in the embedded MAP1 and then calls `bp-add`, which writes
 0xCC over the first byte of that function. In a test that is not a
 breakpoint, it is self-corruption: the program under test would patch its
 own running code to prove a menu item works.

 The breakpoint line answers "Symbol not found: zzz-no-such-function", and
 that string is produced by `bp-add-symbolic` and by nothing else -- had
 the line fallen through to the evaluator it would read `expr: unknown
 name zzz-no-such-function` -- so it still proves the line was ROUTED to
 the symbolic path. Since main 11552 fixed `map1-find` it proves more than
 that: the map really is found and `map1-find-by-name` really is walked,
 which this paragraph used to say no test here could do.

 We say:

Section: Fixtures

  bare-console : UefiConsoleState
  bare-console = UefiConsoleState {
    sys-table = 0,
    con-out = 0,
    con-in = 0,
    rows = 25,
    cols = 80,
    cursor-row = 0,
    cursor-col = 0
  }

  bare-disk : DiskFactStore
  bare-disk = DiskFactStore { superblock = empty-superblock, mem = fact-store-empty, base = 0, limit = 0 }

  bare-keys : Keypair
  bare-keys = generate-keypair (sha256 (text-to-bytes "codex-dev-name-cmd-test"))

Section: Reporting

  report : Text, DevConsoleState -> [Console] Nothing
  report (label) (s) = act
    print-line-uni (label & ": " & s.status)
  end

Section: Body

 The cursor readings are the discriminating ones. After each name is
 consumed, `$` reports where the cursor really is: if a name had fallen
 through to the evaluator, the cursor would have moved or the status would
 carry an expression error. `$` answering the address the FIRST expression
 set is what proves the two names went somewhere else entirely.

  opening : [Console, Device.Port, Device.Block] Nothing = act
    let raw = dev-console-init bare-console bare-disk bare-keys
    in let s0 = __record-set raw "source-index" index-full-dev-surface
    in let d0 = dispatch-main-menu "debug" s0
    in act

      at <- handle-console-input d0 "heap-base + 0x40"
      report "cursor-set" at

      s1 <- handle-console-input at "sym"
      report "sym-armed" s1

      s2 <- handle-console-input s1 "parse"
      report "sym-found" s2

      s3 <- handle-console-input s2 "$"
      report "cursor-after-sym" s3

      s4 <- handle-console-input s3 "sym"
      report "sym-armed-again" s4

      s5 <- handle-console-input s4 "zzz-no-such-symbol"
      report "sym-not-found" s5

      b0 <- handle-console-input s5 "bp-add"
      report "bp-armed" b0

      b1 <- handle-console-input b0 "zzz-no-such-function"
      report "bp-name-reached-lookup" b1

      b2 <- handle-console-input b1 "$"
      report "cursor-after-bp" b2

      e0 <- handle-console-input b2 "$ + 1"
      report "expressions-work-again" e0
    end
  end
